.0163726806640625, 0.0132904052734375, -0.017791748046875, -0.0030078887939453125, -0.00894927978515625, -0.0006203651428222656, -0.0010509490966796875, 0.015655517578125, -0.036651611328125, 0.00826263427734375, 0.038055419921875, 0.0211181640625, 0.0061798095703125, -0.0231781005859375, 0.0018520355224609375, -0.0280609130859375, 0.0206451416015625, -0.0133209228515625, 0.0007219314575195312, -0.0144805908203125, -0.035369873046875, 0.01148223876953125, -0.0152740478515625, 0.01229095458984375, -0.005870819091796875, 0.03265380859375, 0.02398681640625, 0.008575439453125, -0.026458740234375, 0.01131439208984375, 0.0092010498046875, -0.005451202392578125, -0.005596160888671875, 0.0007195472717285156, -0.028594970703125, 0.006378173828125, -0.0309600830078125, -0.01050567626953125, -0.00902557373046875, -0.0193939208984375, 0.00567626953125, -0.05072021484375, 0.0013456344604492188, -0.0194244384765625, -0.0299835205078125, 0.01812744140625, -0.032379150390625, -0.01276397705078125, -0.00707244873046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods, defined here as the consistency and robustness of agent performance across different runs, task orders, and environmental stochasticity, has been critically overlooked. In this work, we define fragility as the susceptibility of agent performance to degrade significantly under variations in task order, random seeds, or environmental conditions. We address the following research questions: (1) How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order? (2) What factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved? To this end, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) and Continual Prompt Memory (CPM).\n\n# Methods\n\n## Experimental Setup\n\nOur methodology broadens the scope of evaluation along two axes. First, we include multiple runs (five per configuration, each with a different random seed) to quantify variance. Second, we randomly shuffle the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Specifically, for each configuration, we generate five random permutations of the task sequence using a uniform random shuffle; due to combinatorial explosion, we do not exhaustively enumerate all possible orderings. Experiments are conducted on two complex multi-step environments: (1) a text-based navigation domain, where tasks involve navigating virtual spaces using natural language commands, and (2) a procedural puzzle-solving suite, where tasks require multi-step logical reasoning to solve puzzles. Each environment comprises 10\u201320 tasks, with difficulty determined by the number of required steps, presence of distractors, and dependency on prior knowledge.\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe evaluate agents using success rate (percentage of tasks completed successfully), average episode length, and memory utilization (number of relevant memory entries retrieved per task). Task difficulty is categorized as easy, medium, or hard based on the minimum number of steps and the presence of long-term dependencies.\n\n## Memory Analysis\n\nTo better understand agent fragility, we manually examine the agents\u2019 memory by reviewing stored trajectories and decision rationales. We use a rubric assessing (a) completeness (whether all relevant information is captured), (b) relevance (whether stored information is pertinent to future tasks), and (c) consistency (whether memory entries are internally coherent and non-contradictory). Two independent annotators scored each memory entry on a 3-point scale for each criterion. Inter-annotator agreement, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements were resolved through discussion.\n\n# Results\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks; stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, as evidenced by standard deviations in success rates reaching up to 18% across runs. Second, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: for example, shuffling tasks led to performance drops of up to 25% compared to the default curriculum order. For instance, in the navigation domain, agents trained on a shuffled order failed to generalize to later tasks that required knowledge from earlier ones, resulting in a 22% lower success rate on hard tasks. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\n# Discussion\n\nWe hypothesize that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility. To validate this, we incorporated more detailed rubrics (explicit task success criteria and step-by-step subgoals) and richer environment feedback (e.g., explicit error messages and state change summaries) into the memory construction process. Concretely, agents were provided with structured templates for memory entries, including fields for goal state, encountered obstacles, and corrective actions. This intervention partially closed the performance degradation observed in previous experiments, reducing the average performance gap by 10\u201312%. However, significant gaps remained: for example, agents continued to fail on tasks requiring nuanced context integration or long-term dependencies, as seen in failure cases where relevant prior experiences were not retrieved or misapplied.\n\nThese findings suggest that other uncharacterized factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient generalization across tasks, or brittleness to environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. For example, in 17% of failed episodes, agents retrieved irrelevant or outdated memory entries, directly leading to suboptimal decisions.\n\n# Recommendations and Future Work\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous and actionable evaluation protocols for self-improving agents. We recommend: (1) reporting results across multiple random seeds and task orderings, (2) including ablation studies on memory content and retrieval strategies, (3) benchmarking against adversarially constructed task sequences, and (4) releasing standardized task suites with explicit difficulty annotations. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as interactive memory editing tools or real-time feedback dashboards, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways. Future work should also analyze memory retrieval failures in detail and develop benchmarks that stress-test agents under varied and adversarial conditions."}}